# Correction Q1: Is the prior config + full render appropriate and representative for this cue? Start with "yes" or "no", then one sentence why.
no, the best prior attempt (middle tile) uses an abrupt pose-to-pose snap for the main flex action and can be improved by creating a more dynamic and expressive sequence.
# Correction Q2: If Correction Q1 is no — how should the **entire** `movements` sequence be changed? Cover pose(s), path/movement structure, timing, and expression across the full clip. If yes, write "n/a".
The motion should start from a relaxed, neutral arm pose, then transition quickly to the high, flexed-bicep pose to create a sharp flexing action, and finally, add a concurrent head turn and torso lift to express pride, holding the final pose.

# Q1: How would a human perform this motion? (One sentence)
A human would start with their arm relaxed, then quickly bend their elbow and raise the forearm to tense the bicep, often followed by a slight puff of the chest and a look towards the flexed muscle.
# Q2: Pose Candidates Comparison. Rank at least 2 pose configurations (torso_height, arm_position, gripper_orientation, head, left_arm, x/y/z) and state the winner and why—do NOT pick presets from gesture names alone.
Pose A: `arm_position: "in"`, `elbow_bended: true`, `gripper_orientation: "vertical"`, `z: 75`. This is a classic, high bicep flex pose. Pose B: `arm_position: "out"`, `elbow_bended: true`, `z: 50`. This is a lower, more outward flex that is less iconic. Winner is Pose A because it brings the arm up and across the body in a way that clearly showcases the bicep.
# Q3: Structure Comparison. Compare 2+ full primitive sequences (e.g., M1: pose > movement > pose vs M2: pose > path > path). State the winner and why—**prioritize movement/path beats that express the cue**, not pose-only tweaks.
M1: `pose` (neutral) -> `pose` (flexed) -> `movement` (head turn + torso lift). This separates the flexing action from the expressive follow-up. M2: `pose` (already flexed) -> `movement` (torso lift). This misses the dynamic action of flexing. M1 is the winner because it shows the entire motion from preparation to action to reaction, making it more readable and expressive.
# Q4: Context Reinforcement. Compare 2+ micro-timing choices (speed, repetition, hold_time, torso/head expression) that make the gesture read stronger and pick the best one.
Timing A: A quick, sharp flex (short duration pose transition) followed by a proud, slower head turn and torso lift with a hold. Timing B: All actions performed at a single, medium speed. Winner is Timing A because the contrast between the sharp flex and the deliberate "admiration" movement adds character and better communicates strength and pride.
